ox from iCloud Drive you can  download them from the web anywhere that  you can interact with files on iOS  there's a way to get them into wolfram  player and on top of that it will  automatically sync with your Wolfram  cloud account if you log in so all of  your Wolfram cloud files will be there  things from Wolfram cloud com or things  that you save to the Wolfram cloud from  a desktop version of Mathematica and  that's the way that I made this  presentation for example because this  app doesn't allow editing it's just a  player so I you know wrote this document  on my laptop saved it 12th and cloud and  just opened it directly and here it is  so I'm gonna take a moment to show a few  of these things I'm going to drop out on  my presentation here I'm going to go  back to the home screen of the app I  have you know one file stored locally on  this device right now and I am logged in  to my Wolfram cloud account and here's  my Wolfram cloud files I can add another  Wolfram cloud account you can have an  arbitrary number of cloud accounts and  they can even be on different cloud  servers so if you are have an account on  the Wolfram cloud com that's where it  goes by default but if you have a  private cloud you can just change the  server that you're logging in to  multiple accounts it's very nice you can  import files from other data providers  on the system so right now I have just a  few of them I'm gonna click on iCloud  drive and I'm going to find a document  that I saved in my documents directory  on my laptop which sinks over iCloud  drive and it's in this folder here it is  I'm just going to tap on it and here it  is on my iPad  so this is a file that I downloaded from  the demonstrations project onto my  computer so we can do this other ways I  can drop out of the Wolfram player app  and I can go to Dropbox and I have some  a CDF file stored here  Dropbox doesn't know how to preview it  but I can export it from Dropbox and it  will ask me how do I want to open it I'm  going to open it in an app I'm gonna  choose wolfram player and there it is  okay and this is another file that I  just got from the demonstrations project  and I saved it to my Dropbox account and  then one more I'm gonna open up my email  here and somebody who happens to have  the identical name as me sent me a CDF  file an email and I want to see what's  in the CDF file so I'm just going to tap  on it and I'm going to choose import  with wolfram player and there we go  here's this file so it's very easy to  get your CDF files from anywhere on your  iPad any cloud storage service into the  app so you can view it and start  interacting with it right away  now I'm going to go back to my  presentation here which is stored on  Wolfram cloud comm alright so in  addition to all the ways directly on the  iPad that you can get files you can also  sync with iTunes and there's an  interface in iTunes where you can add  documents to the wolfram player app from  iTunes on your computer so some of the  nice things about this it supports local  computations so there is a Wolfram  kernel running on my iPad right now that  will handle all of the manipulate stuff  so when you change the sliders and  manipulate and the expressions  re-evaluating that's all happening  locally you don't need to be connected  to the network for this to work  being an iOS app we put a great deal of  attention into getting the right user  interface the right user experience for  this platform so one of the things that  means is that when we have buttons  hyperlinks controls all these sorts of  things  they all have touch targets that are  finger sized so you're not going to be  trying you know really hard to hit the  right thing because it was sized  for a very precise pointing device like  a mouse cursor on a laptop or desktop  all our controls are very large you can  touch them with your finger  we have pinch-to-zoom both in things  like 3d graphics but also in the  notebook as a whole so 